Do not fork an error dialog on iOS.

sys_display_error shows its message by forking and exec'ing /usr/bin/xmessage.
On iOS there is no xmessage, the exec fails, and the child carries on running
the engine's code with half a UIKit inherited from a process it is no longer
part of. It died 134 ms after the fork, inside CoreAnimation's commit:

    _dispatch_main_queue_push <- dispatch_async
    <- -[_UIAfterCACommitQueue _enqueueCommitResponse:forPhase:]
    <- CA::Transaction::commit()  ...  UIApplicationMain

That is a crash report for a problem that was only being *reported* - and it
cost a while to see, because the parent went on running and writing its logs,
so the run looked like a game that crashed while playing.

DEBUG_DISPLAY_ERROR logs the message before calling here, so reporting "not
implemented" loses nothing.

--- source/lib/sysdep/os/unix/unix.cpp
+++ source/lib/sysdep/os/unix/unix.cpp
@@ -98,6 +98,18 @@
 	// since xmessage doesn't do that and it'll get clamped
 	// to the screen width
 
+#if OS_IOS
+	// No xmessage, no second process: iOS lets an app fork but almost nothing
+	// is allowed afterwards without an exec, and the child here inherits half
+	// a UIKit. It crashed 134 ms after the fork, inside CoreAnimation's commit
+	// -- a crash report for a problem that was only being *reported*.
+	//
+	// The message is in the log either way (this function is called from
+	// DEBUG_DISPLAY_ERROR, which logs first), so saying "not implemented" is
+	// both true and enough.
+	return ERI_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
+#endif
+
 	const char* cmd = "/usr/bin/xmessage";
 
 	char buttons[256] = "";
